docs-site/docs/explanation/ui-components.md:53:When a table-backed view is loading data, the *table itself* stays on screen — with its header and columns — and the loading indication is a `Status` in the footer: the shared activity dot (`●`/`○`, the same glyph as the row dots) both for work the router tracks and for a view loading its own data (the view asks the router to animate the dot). The braille spinner is removed from the code, and a guard keeps it out. A body that swapped itself out for a loading screen while loading would visibly lose its header and columns for the duration of every refresh, then regain them — a layout jump on every single reload. As a direct consequence, an "empty" message like "No images found" is only shown once loading has actually finished; otherwise the table would be announcing the absence of results it hasn't even finished looking for yet.
